      "text": "Discussion and implementation\nThe intended contribution is to test whether an activity that asks students to retrieve an answer before\nseeing it improves performance relative to reviewing the same content. The activity changes what\nstudents do with the material, rather than simply increasing their exposure time. The primary\ncomparison remains uncertain, with the reported interval including zero and a positive difference that\ncould matter educationally.\nEight of the 12 class teachers returned a short implementation questionnaire. Seven described the\nactivity as manageable. Preparation involved a 40-minute training session and adapting questions to\nthe week's content; preparation time during the intervention was not measured. These reports concern\nfeasibility for respondents, not all teachers or effects on student motivation.\nSchools adopting this activity should provide preparation time and access to curriculum-aligned\nquestions. This recommendation follows from the tasks required for implementation; it does not depend\non claiming that the trial established superiority. A larger follow-up should estimate the\nbetween-condition difference with appropriate attention to the class-level assignment and a longer\nassessment interval.\nThis is an excerpt supplied for review. The literature review and reference list are not included.\nPublic synthetic review task - page 2\n",
